usation  could be an attempt by the kremlin to  justify such an attack  the u.s house of representatives has  approved a massive spending bill that  includes 13.6 billion dollars of aid to  ukraine and european allies the funds  will cover the cost of sending u.s  troops and weapons to eastern europe as  well as care for  west refugees president kamala harris is  in warsaw for talks with polish leaders  tensions are high after the united  states rejected a proposal from poland  to transfer mig-29 fighter jets to a u.s  air base and then deliver them on to  ukraine after concluding her visit to  poland harris will then head to romania  the united nations says around 2.2  million people have now fled ukraine  most of them are women and children  often leaving loved ones behind to fight  not knowing whether they'll see each  other again  the majority of the refugees are  arriving in poland dw's alexandra von  naman sent us this report from the  western border crossing of signie  one last embrace before they say goodbye  vadim has brought his family to the  polish border so they can leave the  country he will be heading back to the  front lines  it is because of the kids  no one wants to leave their home it's so  hard you can't explain it  it's hard  we don't know  it was so good to have them by my side  when you're together with your family  you feel better  now i'll be alone  but i know they'll be safe  [Music]  and that safety is just a few meters  away we are at the border checkpoint in  shahini the busiest border crossing  between ukraine and poland those who are  now here have been waiting for hours  just like natalya and her twins they  escaped the russian bombardments in  harkev in eastern ukraine  we've been through a lot you're sitting  in the hall or in the cellar with your  children while they're bombing the city  and you don't know  will a bomb drop now and that's it what  did we do to deserve that  natalia doesn't believe that the war  will be over soon many ukrainians seem  to think the same and so the lions at  the border are not getting any shorter  people on the move both inside and  outside the country in desperate need of  protection and support as russia's war  with ukraine continues an estimated 4  million people may flee the country  according to the united nations  hot tea and snacks for free distributed  by ukrainian and international  volunteers along the road to the border  this is where we meet nattella from kiev  she's traveling alone with her three  children  the children are suffering the most  because my husband had to stay in kiev  they cry my older son keeps asking will  we never ever see dad again then he  starts crying again  the u.n has called it the fastest  growing refugee crisis in europe since  world war ii this is what it looks like  in real life  the foreign ministers of russia and  ukraine are in turkey for their first  face-to-face talks since the invasion of  ukraine turkish foreign minister melvut  chavisoldo greeted his ukrainian  counterpart dimitro kuleba and russia's  foreign minister sergey lavrov on the  sidelines of the diplomacy forum in  antalya  solo says the aim is to pave the way for  a meeting between the leaders of russia  in ukraine host turkey is a nato member  but has close ties to both countries  let's get more on this from dw's  political analyst for eastern europe and  russia constantine egert welcome to  constantine what can be expected from  the meeting today  hello anthony the decision essentially  lies with putin  if he's bent on continuing his uh  invasion of ukraine his campaign in  ukraine then basically it will be very  simple lavrov recites the old demands  uh basically demilitarization  recognition of the so-called donbass  republics the recognition of the crimean  annexation no nato in ukraine  kuleva says no everyone leaves  i think that if lavrov is to offer some  modified uh demands then it may be the  beginning of a kind of conversation  after two we